<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arkona</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cape Arkona, Jaromarsburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arkona is a chalk cape on the northern tip of the Baltic island of Rügen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, site of a fortified temple of the Slavic Rani. Its sanctuary of the four-headed god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svantevit">Svantevit</a>, described by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxo-grammaticus">Saxo Grammaticus</a>, was the last great pagan shrine of the Baltic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> until King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-i-of-denmark">Valdemar I of Denmark</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absalon">Absalon</a> captured it in 1168 and destroyed the idol. Ramparts of the stronghold survive on the cliff edge.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the twelfth century the dukes fought Polish rulers, Danish kings and Saxon princes; Danish expeditions under Valdemar I and Bishop Absalon devastated the coast and destroyed the temple fortress of <b>Arkona</b> on Rügen in 1168.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Duchy of Pomerania · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Valdemar I the Great and Bishop Absalon, Danish fleets fought the Wendish pirates of the southern Baltic and in 1168 took <b>Arkona</b> on Rügen, destroying the temple of Svantevit.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1168 <b>Arkona</b> on Rugia surrendered to King Valdemar I of Denmark, and Bishop Absalon destroyed the temple of Svetovid.</blockquote><a href="/religion/slavic-paganism">Slavic paganism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1168 the temple-stronghold of the four-headed Svantevit at <b>Arkona</b> on Rügen surrendered to King Valdemar I of Denmark, and Bishop Absalon destroyed the temple; the last place of Slavic paganism on the maps, a holy place at Kobarid in present-day Slovenia, was stamped out in a “crusade”…</blockquote><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move</a></li><li><blockquote>The Slavic Rani ruled Rügen from the temple fortress of <b>Arkona</b>, sanctuary of the god Svantevit.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-rugen">Principality of Rügen</a></li><li><blockquote>The Rani were ruled by princes, but Saxo Grammaticus and Helmold of Bosau describe the high priest of <b>Arkona</b> as the real power.</blockquote><a href="/state/rani">Rani</a></li></ul></section>
